            <div xml:id="d741">

                <pb facs="0972" n="870" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg870"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/2870: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-12-04T14:00:00+01:00">December 4, 1935—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 4—10:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>638. Department’s 141, December 3, 9 a.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">December 2, 8
                        p.m.</hi>] and 135, November 21, 2 p.m.;<anchor n="79" xml:id="d741-f1"/>
                    Consulate’s No. 625, November 29, 2 p.m.; and despatch 1473 (political) October
                        30.<anchor n="80" xml:id="d741-f2"/> I have knowledge that Vasconcellos
                    derives what he believes to be American policy from unofficial sources which are
                    in certain aspects definitely misleading. I thus feel that it might be useful to
                    seek an occasion to call to his attention Washington’s position as expressed in
                    recent official statements in a manner similar to that in the case of
                    Avenol.</p>
                <p>Please instruct.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
